Package: upcie-experimental-dkms
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, dkms
Provides: dmabuf-import-dkms, iommu-map-pa-dkms
Conflicts: dmabuf-import-dkms, iommu-map-pa-dkms,
 udmabuf-import-dkms, upcie-iommu-map-dkms
Replaces: dmabuf-import-dkms, iommu-map-pa-dkms,
 udmabuf-import-dkms, upcie-iommu-map-dkms
Description: experimental out-of-tree modules used by upcie (DKMS)
 Both out-of-tree kernel modules the experimental upcie paths need, built and
 installed via DKMS so they rebuild automatically on kernel updates with no
 custom kernel required. They are unlocked together because the paths that use
 them need both; each remains a separate module with its own author, licence
 and rationale, documented in /usr/src/upcie-experimental-<version>/.
 .
 dmabuf_import serves the dma-buf import ioctls (DMABUF_IMPORT_ATTACH /
 DMABUF_IMPORT_DETACH / DMABUF_IMPORT_GET_MAP) on /dev/dmabuf_import. It imports
 an external dma-buf, whoever exported it, and shares its DMA addresses with
 userspace.
 .
 iommu_map_pa serves the IOMMU_MAP_PA / IOMMU_UNMAP_PA ioctls on
 /dev/iommu_map_pa. Given a PCI BDF, an array of physical addresses and a
 userspace-chosen IOVA base, it installs the addresses into the IOMMU domain
 the device currently uses, which is what makes GPU memory reachable for P2P
 DMA from a VFIO-controlled NVMe.
 .
 Both are experimental. iommu_map_pa in particular borrows a live IOMMU domain
 it does not own and bypasses VFIO and iommufd IOVA accounting; its device node
 is root-only because mapping arbitrary physical addresses for a DMA-capable
 device is equivalent to raw physical memory access.
 .
 Neither module has hardware to match on, so nothing would autoload them.
 Installing the package therefore loads both and arranges for them to load on
 boot, which is what makes the experimental paths work without further steps.
 Mask /usr/lib/modules-load.d/upcie-experimental.conf to stop that.
